# Fictional EP Tracklist

## 1. Gate Two Holds Both Reeds

**Story & Context:** At 108 BPM, Eleni begins with both chanters in a tight LOCK over Luc’s one-bar kick/snare loop and short tonic sub. Sofia places one prepared-electric-piano fifth every four bars. After sixteen bars, Eleni changes one fingering while maintaining bag pressure; the sound enters SHEAR and then VEER as one reed traces a narrow descending figure while the other remains almost fixed. Luc removes hats and sub during the widest separation. Nikos replaces the expected snare with one dry toumbi rim hit. Amélie enters only after REJOIN with a low bass-clarinet fundamental. The second half repeats the same electronic pattern but uses a different reed pair, making the acoustic instability noticeably faster without any tempo change.

**Visual & Lyrics Prompt:** Closed ferry gate marked 2, two parallel reed lines drawn on a sailing schedule, initially touching and then separating before rejoining near the bottom of the page. Tsampouna chanters and a blue plastic chair in shallow focus. Sparse vocal fragments limited to “gate two,” “hold both,” “one moves,” and “join again.”

## 2. North Wind Splits the Ticket Line

**Story & Context:** At 116 BPM, the band uses the EP’s hardest New Beat pulse. Luc keeps kick on all four quarters but drops the snare for one full bar whenever Eleni enters VEER. Eleni performs short paired-reed cells whose degree of separation changes through Bag Pressure Drift rather than different notes. Sofia holds one clipped minor-seventh dyad as a fixed reference before each pressure swell. Nikos alternates machine clap replacements between frame-drum palm strokes and toumbi stick taps. Amélie stays out until the central break, where the sub vanishes and she holds one chalumeau pedal beneath a long rough-unison REJOIN. The track demonstrates that dynamic pressure alone can make the same fingering feel stable, frayed, or aggressive.

**Visual & Lyrics Prompt:** Wind notice pinned beside a ferry ticket grille, two black queue lines on the floor diverging under a strong draft, one goatskin bag and paired chanters visible behind the grille. Sparse fragments limited to “north wind,” “same fingers,” “pressure higher,” and “line divides.”

## 3. Last Sailing Rejoins After Midnight

**Story & Context:** The closer runs at 102 BPM and begins with no sub bass. Eleni holds a low, rough LOCK while Luc uses only kick, clipped snare, and one closed hat every two beats. Sofia plays isolated open fifths. Nikos adds one muted frame-drum stroke every four bars. Each eight-bar section pushes the paired reeds farther into VEER before returning. Amélie replaces the absent sub with soft low fundamentals during the widest divergences. In the final minute, Eleni gradually relaxes bag pressure until the beating between the two reeds slows and the pipes settle into their tightest REJOIN of the record. Only then does Luc introduce one final short sub note. The band stops immediately after it, leaving no reverb tail.

